<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monte Carlo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monte Carlo is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> on the Spélugues plateau east of the harbour. Prince Charles III founded its casino in 1863 to restore the principality's finances after the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> and Roquebrune, and the quarter, named after him in 1866, grew into a resort of the European elite. It is known for the Casino, the Hôtel de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the Monaco Grand Prix.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monte-carlo.jpg" alt="Monte Carlo" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tobi 87 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_von_Monaco-La_Turbie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means Mount Charles. Strictly it belongs to one ward of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a>, Monte Carlo/Spélugues, but it is also used for a larger district, the Monte Carlo Quarter, which corresponds to the former municipality and includes the wards of La Rousse/Saint Roman, Larvotto/Bas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulins">Moulins</a> and Saint Michel. Monaco has four traditional quarters: Fontvieille, the newest, Monaco-Ville, the oldest, La Condamine and Monte Carlo. The ward covers only 28.14 hectares, on a prominent escarpment at the base of the Maritime Alps, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. At its eastern border one crosses into the French town of Beausoleil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1856 Charles III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> granted a concession to Napoleon Langlois and Albert Aubert to set up a sea-bathing facility for the treatment of various diseases and to build a German-style casino. The first casino opened in La Condamine and did not succeed. It moved several times before reaching its present site in the area called Les Spélugues, the Caves. Success came slowly, largely because Monaco was inaccessible from much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the railway of 1868 brought an influx of visitors and growing wealth. Charles III also established the Hôtel de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1864, and the church of Saint-Charles was completed in 1883.</p><p>The Salle Garnier, the casino's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and home of the Opéra de Monte-Carlo, was designed by Charles Garnier, who also designed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> opera house. It was inaugurated on 25 January 1879 with a performance by Sarah Bernhardt dressed as a nymph, and the first opera performed there was Robert Planquette's Le Chevalier Gaston on 8 February 1879. Tramways served the quarter from 1898 to 1931. In 1911 the constitution divided the principality into three municipalities, one of them Monte Carlo. They were merged in 1917, after accusations that the government used them to divide and conquer, and have been wards since.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Monte Carlo centres on the Place du Casino, with the casino, the Hôtel de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the Café de Paris and the Salle Garnier. In the east, Larvotto has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a>'s only public beach and the Grimaldi Forum convention centre. Much of the Circuit de Monaco, on which the Monaco Grand Prix is run, lies within the quarter. The Monte Carlo Rally is run mostly on French roads, and the Monte Carlo Masters tennis tournament is held in the neighbouring French commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roquebrune-cap-martin">Roquebrune-Cap-Martin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monte Carlo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monégasque citizens are forbidden to enter the <b>Monte Carlo</b> casino unless they work there, a rule decreed so as not to overtax them; by 1869 the casino's earnings let the principality stop collecting income tax from its citizens altogether, a policy that still stands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
